 Cones" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124697 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124697 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124697 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124697 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "665ce843-c897-5cef-9141-25ecb7cdc260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124893 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=(z-4)^{2}\\,?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fafc510-161c-5c1c-82ac-429f0c08613a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124893 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =(z-4)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =(z-4)^{2} \\\\ \\pm \\frac{y}{2} & =z-4 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{y}{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two lines in the $yz$-plane with equations $z=\\pm \\frac{y}{2}+4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0cabaaa-77b2-5df5-b276-82aad1e2675a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125238 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4} & =(0+3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4}{4} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1 & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125513 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125513 #edb/ref 1061 30715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125513 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8afa0f97-79d7-5556-8054-cf7a927fbc25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125513 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125514 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125514 #edb/ref 1061 30716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125514 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b83d2704-1cd8-5d3e-a5d6-3347b6842a7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125514 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125515 #edb/ref 1061 30717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125515 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60b53775-0c8d-50d6-8793-5131aef5e1ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125515 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125516 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125516 #edb/ref 1061 30720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125516 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "63150029-88d0-527b-9d21-8af46b91197c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125516 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125640 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125640 #edb/ref 1061 45285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125640 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8cf188e-213a-5603-a9b9-fc97304c8d5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125640 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125828 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the quadric surface\n\n$$z^2 = x^2 + y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\nThis is an example of an **elliptic cone**. It has its **vertex** at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the cone.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic cone whose vertex is at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$(z-z_0)^2 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125828 #edb/ref 1065 4924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125828 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf9fab37-b71c-5d84-95d5-791cd41706a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125828 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125829 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we've encountered cones whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when a cone is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic cone centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y^2$ term is isolated on one side, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\ny^2 = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06c02be2e3ee96862abacc19885e34a8b3cb096a1b85417169022394839e7888.png)\n\n- When the $x^2$ term is isolated on one side, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\nx^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/0e/0ef83c7e023ddcf5e408c7b8cb4e97deb7ece2f366628e019aa04ef90810c6f9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125829 #edb/ref 1065 4925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125829 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3ba615cd-207c-53f0-ac45-57c3b3f46b84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125829 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125882 #edb/ref 1064 "The traces of an elliptic cone are either intersecting lines, ellipses, hyperbolas, or points.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic cone given by\n\n$$\nz^2 = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/ba/ba552154b10ae7919496031613005c60461272923c6cae262a757b6df89c398d.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/2e/2e22ee99e93475eabae9ce8caacee814d0066dd30042367668542485cdacd8a3.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k \\neq 0$ is an ellipse:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = k^2\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d32e0c05b0bd5a55ff38183b362eebd44bf668dc9ee68ec5b6ae2274fdae934e.png)\n\n- The traces of our surface with the plane $x=k$ or $y=k$ for $k \\neq 0$ are hyperbolas:\n\n$$\nz^2-y^2 = k^2 \\qquad \\textrm{or}\\qquad z^2-x^2 = k^2\n$$\n\n![](images/23/23dd8112391406afd1da714df32d3a01ddb99b68c3b0885078ad76ebba23f9b5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125882 #edb/ref 1065 7841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125882 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "efd10d7e-d93a-5f24-b896-67f70dc4aa16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125882 #edb/ref 1067 "Traces of Elliptic Cones" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281855 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,-8,0)$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11956820-87e5-59f7-9a01-587e112f1f0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281880 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1451ba15-6566-5085-9e0b-ef6f6d77f7f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281909 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,-8,0)$ and $(0,-2,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281909 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281909 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "187b372f-12c8-5b94-b30e-c60e9d7967cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282117 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3063bb6b-93ae-58fc-87f3-0367fab1c670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282230 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,-8,0)$ and $(0,7,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282230 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282230 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e0589bc-5681-5f2e-a140-09e251104353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282661 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282661 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282661 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71a8465f-bb0b-559f-98ac-0c12856b902a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282703 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,-2,0)$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282703 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282703 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "767e5e0e-9067-5c62-b815-f338c0ec4581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282854 #edb/ref 1080 "$(0,-8,0)$ and $(0,2,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86fc7800-f4b4-55ec-9a55-aab7e674de5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282897 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282897 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282897 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8bb653b9-298d-5248-b2b6-d34110522d1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283901 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283901 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283901 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc7e7291-2544-5ff2-8bdc-d7c677dfff07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186282661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284236 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ae110007-f697-5fc7-ac5d-1ec61fa80379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186281909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284245 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b2c4aa53-f578-528f-b782-49fe2162ccf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284746 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284746 #edb/ref 1069 "q-176833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284746 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284746 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x+8)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3}=\\frac{(z-7)^{2}}{7},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284746 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "235b5226-9bea-5c1e-801c-25a2ec82bb10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284746 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+8)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =\\frac{(0-7)^{2}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{64}{16}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =\\frac{49}{7} \\\\ 4+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{6} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =3 \\\\ (y+5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-5\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-8,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-8,0)$ and $(0,-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284747 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284747 #edb/ref 1069 "q-176835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284747 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284747 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the intersection of the elliptic cone $4(x+3)^{2}+8(y-1)^{2}=(z+7)^{2}$ and the plane $z=-3$ is an ellipse, what is the length of its major axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e552ce94-8cbc-51a4-8c04-3b8e44dddcc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284747 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the intersection of the elliptic cone and the plane $z=-3,$ we substitute $z=-3$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+3)^{2}+8(y-1)^{2} & =(-3+7)^{2} \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}+8(y-1)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}+8(y-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{(2)^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with a semi-major axis of length $a=2.$ Therefore, the major axis has length $2a=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321164 #edb/ref 1080 "z=\\pm \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2}}+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321164 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321164 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "017f